.TH ATD 8 "2026" "spicule" "spicule System Commands"
.SH NAME
atd \- run jobs queued by at and batch
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B atd
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B atd
is the background daemon that actually executes jobs submitted with
.BR at (1)
or
.BR batch (1).
Submitting a job only writes it to a spool directory and returns
immediately; nothing runs until an instance of
.B atd
is running to notice it. Unlike the other utilities in this collection,
.B atd
is a standalone program only: it is not available as a shell built\-in,
since it is meant to run indefinitely as its own long\-lived process
rather than return control to whatever started it.
.PP
Once running,
.B atd
repeatedly polls the job spool. On each poll, every job whose scheduled
time has arrived is started, except that a job submitted to the
.RB \` b \'
queue
.RB ( batch (1)'s
own queue) is deferred to a later poll whenever the system's one\-minute
load average is at or above 1.5, approximating
.BR batch (1p) 's
"run using algorithms, based on unspecified factors" requirement. On a
platform where a load average cannot be determined at all, batch\-queue
jobs are simply run as soon as they are due, since there is no busyness
signal available to act on.
.PP
A due job is run through the shell, with its standard input taken from
an empty source and its combined standard output and standard error
captured to a file next to the job in the spool, for the submitting
user to read afterward \(em there being no mail transport to fulfill
.BR at (1p)/ batch (1p)'s
traditional "mail the output" behavior. Multiple jobs due in the same
poll are all started without waiting for one another, and a
long\-running job never delays another due job, or the entirely
separate
.BR crond (1)
process, from noticing its own due entries.
.PP
On receipt of
.B SIGTERM
or
.BR SIGINT ,
.B atd
finishes the poll currently in progress, waits for and reaps any jobs
it has already started, and exits; it does not kill jobs that are
still running.
.PP
On the native\-Linux backend, if standard input is a terminal when
.B atd
starts, it detaches from it (forking, starting a new session, and
redirecting standard input, output, and error to a null device) so
that it keeps running after the shell that launched it exits. If
.B atd
is already started without a controlling terminal attached, no such
detachment happens. On the Windows backend there is no controlling\-
terminal concept to detach from at all; an invocation of
.B atd
there is simply an ordinary, long\-running process for as long as
whatever started it keeps it alive.
.B atd
is not registered as a Windows service by this implementation; running
it as one, if desired, is left to an external service wrapper.
.SH OPERANDS
None.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B SPICULE_ATD_POLL_MS
Overrides the poll interval, in milliseconds (default 1000). Ignored
unless it parses as a positive integer. Since
.BR at (1p)/ batch (1p)
jobs have no specification\-mandated latency, this exists purely to
speed up testing; it is not needed for ordinary use.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
.B atd
ran until it received
.B SIGTERM
or
.B SIGINT
and shut down normally.
.TP
.B 1
The job spool directory could not be created or accessed.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR at (1),
.BR batch (1),
.BR crond (1),
.BR crontab (1)
